const units=[{name:'Introduction to databases and data models',hours:6,short:'Foundations',source:'Unit 1.ppt'},{name:'Relational data model',hours:8,short:'Relational model',source:'Unit 2 ER slides + Unit2 Keys & Relational algebra.pptx'},{name:'SQL and normalization',hours:8,short:'SQL & normalization',source:'module 3 sql and normalisation.ppt'},{name:'Transaction management and concurrency control',hours:6,short:'Transactions',source:'Mod 4 DBMS.pdf; Unit 1 transaction slides; supplementary syllabus concepts'}];
// unit | title | priority | definition | bullet points (~ separates) | example | exam tip | recall question | recall answer | MCQ options (correct first) | explanation
const raw=`1|DBMS: definition, characteristics and applications|Core|A DBMS is software that defines, stores, retrieves, updates and controls a database. A database is an organized collection of related data.|Self-describing catalog stores metadata.~Data sharing supports multiple applications and views.~Constraints, authorization, recovery and transactions support reliable access.~Applications include banking, university registration, railway booking and retail.|A college stores Student, Course and Enrollment in one managed database; a registration transaction validates the course and records enrollment.|For a 5-mark answer give definition, four characteristics and one application.|What is metadata?|Data about the structure, types and constraints of data.|Data describing data~A backup copy~A row identifier only~A SQL query|Metadata includes schema and constraints, not merely stored records.
1|File system versus DBMS|PYQ|File processing stores application-specific files, while a DBMS provides shared structured access and centralized control.|Redundancy causes inconsistent copies; DBMS reduces avoidable duplication.~File programs need custom retrieval; SQL supports ad hoc queries.~Integrity and security rules are enforced centrally.~Atomicity, recovery and concurrency control reduce incomplete or conflicting updates.~DBMS introduces overhead, cost and administrative complexity.|If a student changes address, separate department files may disagree; a shared Student relation keeps one authoritative value.|Write a comparison table with redundancy, access, isolation, integrity, atomicity, concurrency and security. Include the address or bank transfer example.|Why is data redundancy undesirable?|Duplicate values can waste storage and become inconsistent.|Controlled sharing and constraints~No need for security~Always zero redundancy~No processing overhead|A DBMS controls redundancy and sharing but does not eliminate every duplicate or cost.
1|Database users and DBA|Core|Different users interact with a database through applications, query tools or administration interfaces.|Naive or parametric users use predefined forms (bank teller).~Application programmers write programs that access data.~Sophisticated users submit queries directly.~Specialized users build unusual database applications.~DBA defines schema, grants authorization, manages storage, backups, tuning and recovery.|A student uses the portal; a programmer builds it; a DBA controls the database permissions and backups.|List user types with an example and DBA responsibilities.|Who manages privileges and backups?|Database administrator (DBA).|DBA~Naive user~Customer~Tuple|The DBA manages authorization and routine maintenance.
1|Schema, instance and metadata|Core|Schema is the relatively stable design; instance is the actual contents at a particular time.|Relation schema specifies attributes, domains and constraints.~Database instance changes with insert, delete and update.~A valid instance satisfies the schema constraints.~Intension = schema; extension = current contents.~Data dictionary stores metadata.|STUDENT(RollNo, Name, Marks) is a schema. {(101,Asha,85),(102,Rahul,72)} is one instance.|Use a table structure and two different snapshots to show the distinction.|Does inserting a row normally change the schema?|No; it changes the instance.|Instance~Schema~Data model~External mapping|An insertion changes stored tuples, not the table definition.
1|Three levels of abstraction and data independence|PYQ|Abstraction hides details at external/view, conceptual/logical and internal/physical levels. Independence means changes at a lower schema level need not alter the higher level.|View: user-specific subset, e.g. student name and marks.~Logical: overall relations, keys and constraints.~Physical: files, blocks, indexes, compression.~Logical independence: conceptual changes need not affect external views or applications.~Physical independence: internal storage changes need not affect conceptual schema. Logical is generally harder.|Add an index without changing STUDENT: physical independence. Add a new attribute while preserving existing views: logical independence.|Draw three boxes with the two independence boundaries labeled.|Which independence is illustrated by changing an index?|Physical data independence.|Physical data independence~Logical data independence~Entity integrity~View serializability|Indexes are internal storage/access structures.
1|DBMS system architecture|Core|The query processor interprets and executes queries; storage manager coordinates access to stored data.|DDL interpreter records definitions in catalog.~DML compiler parses and optimizes queries into plans.~Query evaluation engine executes plans.~Storage manager includes buffer, file, authorization/integrity and transaction managers.~Disk stores data files, indexes, catalog and statistics.|SELECT Name FROM Student WHERE RollNo=101 → parse/optimize → execute → buffer/file access → return rows.|Draw Users → Query processor → Storage manager → Disk. Explain each component.|What does the buffer manager do?|Loads disk pages into memory and manages cached pages.|Query evaluation engine~Data dictionary~End user~External schema|The evaluation engine executes the selected query plan.
1|Two-tier and three-tier architecture|Core|Two-tier clients connect directly to database server; three-tier clients use an intermediate application server.|Two-tier: client application contains much business logic, uses ODBC/JDBC or driver.~Three-tier: presentation, application/business logic, database tiers.~Three-tier centralizes validation and can improve security and scalability.~Two-tier is simpler for small deployments; three-tier adds network and deployment complexity.|Desktop client → DB server; browser → application API → DB server.|Do not confuse deployment tiers with three-schema abstraction levels.|What separates clients from the database in three-tier architecture?|Application server / business logic layer.|Application server~Data dictionary~Foreign key~Index|The middle tier mediates database access.
1|Data models: hierarchical, network, relational and others|PYQ|A data model describes data structure, relationships, semantics and constraints.|Hierarchical: rooted tree, each child has one parent; fast predictable paths but rigid many-to-many support.~Network: graph-like links allow multiple parents, flexible but navigational and complex.~Relational: tables linked by keys; declarative queries and flexible relationships.~ER: conceptual entities and relationships.~Object-oriented: objects with identity, state and methods; object-relational extends tables.~Semi-structured: XML/JSON documents with potentially varying fields.|College → Department → Student is a tree; a student taking courses from multiple departments is awkward in a strict tree.|For hierarchical model: diagram, example, advantages (simple tree, fast traversal), limitations (rigid, redundancy for M:N, path dependence).|How many parents can a child have in a strict hierarchical model?|One parent (except root).|One~Unlimited~Exactly two~No parent ever|The root has none; non-root child records have one parent.
2|ER entities, entity types and sets|Core|An entity is a distinguishable object; entity type defines common structure; entity set is its current collection.|Strong entity has its own identifying key.~Weak entity lacks an independent full key; owner key + partial key identifies it.~Weak entity has total participation in its identifying relationship.~Physical and conceptual objects can be entities.~Relationships represent associations and may have attributes.|EMPLOYEE(EmpID) owns DEPENDENT(Name); key of DEPENDENT is (EmpID,Name), assuming name unique per employee.|Draw double rectangle for weak entity and double diamond for identifying relationship.|What identifies a weak entity?|Owner primary key plus the weak entity partial key.|Owner key plus partial key~Partial key alone always~Any non-key attribute~Relationship name|A weak entity is identified within its owner context.
2|ER attribute types and Chen notation|Core|Attributes describe entities or relationships and may have different structures.|Simple: atomic; composite: decomposable (Address → City, Street).~Single-valued: one value; multivalued: several (Phone).~Stored: BirthDate; derived: Age.~Key attribute underlined; partial key dashed underline.~Chen notation: rectangle entity, diamond relationship, oval attribute, double oval multivalued, dashed oval derived.~NULL means unknown or inapplicable, not zero.|STUDENT has RollNo (key), Name (composite), Phone (multivalued), BirthDate (stored), Age (derived).|Label all attribute types neatly. Map multivalued attributes to a separate relation.|What is the notation for a multivalued attribute?|Double oval.|Double oval~Dashed oval~Double diamond~Single rectangle|Double oval denotes a multivalued attribute.
2|Relationship cardinality and participation|Core|Cardinality describes maximum associations; participation describes minimum associations.|1:1 each side at most one; 1:N one parent can relate to many children.~M:N many on both sides; use an associative relation in mapping.~Total participation = every entity must participate (double line).~Partial participation = optional (single line).~Min-max notation (0,N), (1,1) states minimum and maximum.~Unary/recursive, binary and ternary describe relationship degree.|Employee works for exactly one department: Employee (1,1), Department (0,N).|State both cardinality and participation; do not assume all relationships are mandatory.|What is total participation?|Every entity participates in at least one relationship instance.|Minimum participation is one~Maximum is one~All attributes are keys~All links are many-to-many|Total is a minimum constraint, independent of maximum.
2|Relational model and schemas|Core|A relation is a set of tuples over named attributes and their domains. A relational database is a collection of relations.|Tuple = row; attribute = column; domain = permitted atomic values.~Degree = number of attributes; cardinality = number of tuples.~Relational model has no duplicate tuples and no inherent row order.~A relation schema defines the structure, while relation state is the current set of tuples.~SQL tables and query outputs can permit duplicates.|STUDENT(RollNo, Name, Dept, Marks) with 4 rows has degree 4, cardinality 4.|Differentiate relation cardinality (row count) from ER relationship cardinality.|What is degree of a relation?|Number of attributes (columns).|Number of columns~Number of rows~Number of foreign keys~Number of databases|Degree is the number of attributes in the schema.
2|Types of keys|PYQ|A superkey uniquely identifies tuples; a candidate key is a minimal superkey; a primary key is a selected candidate key.|Alternate keys are candidate keys not selected.~Composite key uses multiple attributes; all must be necessary for a candidate key.~Foreign key references a primary or eligible unique key, including in the same table.~Primary key cannot be NULL; foreign key may be NULL if permitted.~Uniqueness must hold in all valid states, not just sample rows.|STUDENT(ID,RollNo,Email,Name), assuming ID, RollNo, Email each unique: candidate keys ID/RollNo/Email, choose ID as PK; ID+Name is a nonminimal superkey.|For ENROLLMENT(StudentID,CourseID,Semester), if repeats across semesters are allowed, use all three as key.|Why is {ID, Name} not a candidate key if ID alone is unique?|Name is redundant; the superkey is not minimal.|It is not minimal~It cannot be unique~It is always a foreign key~It contains NULL by definition|A candidate key must be minimal under set inclusion.
2|Integrity constraints|PYQ|Integrity constraints are rules that every valid database state must satisfy.|Domain: legal types/ranges, e.g. Marks integer 0–100.~Key: candidate key values unique.~Entity integrity: primary key attributes never NULL.~Referential integrity: a non-null foreign key must match a referenced key; null allowed when permitted.~Semantic: business rules, often CHECK or triggers.|Enrollment.StudentID=101 must refer to existing Student(101); CHECK(Marks BETWEEN 0 AND 100).|PYQ asks key, domain and referential integrity; define each, show schema and one valid/invalid value.|Which constraint prevents an enrollment referencing a nonexistent student?|Referential integrity.|Referential integrity~Domain only~Entity abstraction~Physical independence|Foreign key enforcement checks references.
2|ER to relational schema mapping|PYQ|Mapping transforms conceptual entities and relationships into tables with keys and constraints.|Strong entity → table of simple attributes; flatten composite, normally omit derived.~Weak entity → table with owner PK as FK plus partial key in PK.~1:1 → FK on preferably total side, UNIQUE and NOT NULL where required.~1:N → FK of 1-side in N-side table; add relationship attributes there.~M:N → junction table with both FKs and appropriate composite PK.~Multivalued attribute → separate owner key + value relation; n-ary → separate relation with participating keys and constraints.|Professor(PID PK), Course(CID PK, PID FK), Student(SID PK), Enrollment(SID FK,CID FK, PK(SID,CID)).|Always justify FK placement using cardinality. Explicitly state assumptions for repeat enrollments.|Where is the foreign key in a 1:N relationship?|On the many side.|Many side~Always one side~Neither table~Only in a view|A many-side tuple references one parent.
2|Relational algebra: unary operations|PYQ|Relational algebra is procedural; its inputs and outputs are relations.|Selection σ filters rows by predicate.~Projection π selects attributes and removes duplicate tuples.~Rename ρ changes relation or attribute names.~Operators compose; σ before π when predicate needs omitted attributes.~Use parentheses and attribute subscripts in exam notation.|Student: (101,Asha,EXTC,85),(102,Rahul,IT,72),(103,Sneha,EXTC,90). π_Name(σ_Marks>80(Student)) gives Asha, Sneha.|Write the expression AND resulting relation, including column headers.|Which operator removes duplicate projected rows?|Projection (π), in classical relational algebra.|π projection~σ selection~ρ rename~× Cartesian product|A relation is a set, so projected duplicates collapse.
2|Relational algebra: set operations and joins|PYQ|Union, intersection and difference compare union-compatible relations; product combines all tuple pairs.|Union ∪: distinct tuples in either relation.~Intersection ∩: tuples in both.~Difference −: tuples only in first; not commutative.~Union compatibility: same degree and compatible corresponding domains (rename if needed).~Product ×: m×n tuples, combined attributes; rename clashes.~Theta join = selection over product; natural join matches common names; division can express 'all' queries (extra).|A={1,2,3}, B={3,4,5}: union {1,2,3,4,5}, intersection {3}, difference A−B={1,2}.|For common course names, project names first; different course IDs would stop entire tuples matching.|If two relations contain 3 and 2 tuples, how many product tuples?|6.|6~5~3~2|Cartesian product pairs every tuple from one relation with every tuple from the other.
3|SQL overview and command categories|Core|SQL is a declarative language for defining, querying, modifying and controlling relational databases.|DDL: CREATE, ALTER, DROP, TRUNCATE (dialect dependent).~DML: INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE; SELECT often classified as DQL, or retrieval DML.~DCL: GRANT, REVOKE.~TCL: COMMIT, ROLLBACK, SAVEPOINT.~Types: CHAR fixed, VARCHAR variable, INT, NUMERIC(p,s), DATE.~Use single quotes for string literals; identifiers are distinct from literals.|CREATE TABLE Student(ID INT PRIMARY KEY, Name VARCHAR(40)); INSERT INTO Student VALUES(1,'Asha');|Compare DELETE (rows with optional WHERE), TRUNCATE (all rows, dialect transaction behavior), DROP (object).|Which SQL category includes GRANT and REVOKE?|DCL.|DCL~DDL~TCL~DQL|DCL controls access permissions.
3|DDL and SQL integrity constraints|PYQ|DDL defines database objects, attribute domains and integrity rules.|CREATE TABLE defines types and constraints; ALTER changes structure; DROP removes object.~PRIMARY KEY enforces unique non-null identity; UNIQUE constrains duplicates (NULL semantics vary).~NOT NULL rejects missing values; CHECK constrains allowed values (UNKNOWN can pass).~FOREIGN KEY references eligible key; ON DELETE CASCADE/SET NULL controls changes.~DEFAULT supplies omitted values; use NOT NULL plus CHECK if NULL must be prohibited.|CREATE TABLE Student(ID INT PRIMARY KEY, Name VARCHAR(40) NOT NULL, Marks INT CHECK(Marks BETWEEN 0 AND 100), DeptID INT REFERENCES Department(DeptID));|For SQL constraints give syntax and rejected example; choose valid referenced table first.|Why combine NOT NULL and CHECK for a required marks field?|CHECK alone may accept UNKNOWN from NULL.|CHECK can accept UNKNOWN~CHECK forces uniqueness~NOT NULL allows duplicates only~Foreign keys require it always|SQL CHECK generally rejects FALSE, not UNKNOWN.
3|SELECT, WHERE, DISTINCT and string operations|PYQ|SELECT retrieves columns from relations; WHERE filters rows; DISTINCT removes duplicate output rows.|Use AND, OR, NOT; parentheses clarify precedence.~BETWEEN is inclusive; IN tests membership.~LIKE uses % for zero or more characters and _ for exactly one.~IS NULL tests missing values; = NULL is incorrect.~ORDER BY ASC/DESC determines output order.~UPPER, LOWER, LENGTH, SUBSTR and concatenation vary by dialect.|SELECT DISTINCT name FROM Student WHERE marks>80 AND name LIKE 'A%' ORDER BY name;|For date comparisons use ISO date literals (SQLite text dates) or DATE '2017-08-01' in Oracle.|Which pattern finds names starting with A?|LIKE 'A%'.|A%~%A~_A~%A%|The wildcard follows A, so A must be at the beginning.
3|DML: insert, update and delete|PYQ|DML changes the tuples in a relation.|INSERT INTO T(columns) VALUES(...).~UPDATE T SET col=value WHERE condition; no WHERE affects all rows.~DELETE FROM T WHERE condition; no WHERE deletes all rows but leaves schema.~Constraints apply to changes; use transaction boundaries to undo before commit where supported.~CASE can update values conditionally in one statement.|UPDATE Employee SET city='Konkan' WHERE empname='Amruta';|Always verify WHERE and quote text. Mention rollback is possible for uncommitted transactional DML.|Which command removes rows but keeps the table definition?|DELETE.|DELETE~DROP~CREATE~GRANT|DELETE operates on tuples rather than the table definition.
3|Aggregates, GROUP BY and HAVING|PYQ|Aggregates summarize rows; GROUP BY forms groups and HAVING filters group results.|COUNT(*) counts rows; COUNT(column) counts non-null values; COUNT(DISTINCT col) counts distinct non-null values.~SUM, AVG, MIN, MAX generally ignore NULL; empty input returns NULL except COUNT.~WHERE filters before grouping; HAVING filters after grouping.~Nonaggregated selected columns normally belong in GROUP BY.~Logical order: FROM/JOIN → WHERE → GROUP BY → HAVING → SELECT → DISTINCT → ORDER BY.|SELECT dept, AVG(marks) AS avg_marks FROM Student GROUP BY dept HAVING AVG(marks)>75;|Use HAVING for aggregate predicates; include grouping column in SELECT.|Which clause filters groups on AVG(salary)>50000?|HAVING.|HAVING~WHERE~ORDER BY~DISTINCT|HAVING evaluates aggregate results after groups are formed.
3|SQL joins|PYQ|Joins combine rows using a relationship or comparison predicate.|INNER returns matched pairs only.~LEFT returns all left rows plus matching right rows; unmatched columns NULL.~RIGHT preserves right rows; FULL preserves both sides.~CROSS produces all pairs; SELF uses two aliases of same table.~NATURAL joins on every same-named column; explicit ON is safer.~A WHERE condition on the nullable right side can remove unmatched LEFT JOIN rows.|SELECT s.name,d.dept_name FROM Student s LEFT JOIN Department d ON s.dept_id=d.dept_id;|Write aliases, qualified columns and ON condition. Decide which unmatched rows should survive.|Which join preserves every row from the left table?|LEFT OUTER JOIN.|LEFT OUTER JOIN~INNER JOIN~CROSS JOIN~RIGHT OUTER JOIN|LEFT JOIN preserves unmatched left rows using NULLs.
3|SQL set operations and NULL|Core|SQL set operators combine compatible query results. NULL represents unknown or inapplicable data.|UNION removes duplicates; UNION ALL keeps them.~INTERSECT returns common rows; EXCEPT (Oracle MINUS) returns first-only rows.~Corresponding result columns need compatible types and same count.~Comparisons with NULL give UNKNOWN; WHERE keeps TRUE only.~NOT IN can return UNKNOWN if subquery has NULL; NOT EXISTS is often safer.~SQL uses bag semantics by default, unlike classical relational algebra.|SELECT city FROM Employee UNION SELECT city FROM Company; SELECT * FROM Student WHERE marks IS NULL;|Mention Oracle MINUS versus SQLite EXCEPT; INTERSECT ALL and EXCEPT ALL not universally supported.|Which operator retains duplicate rows from both SELECT queries?|UNION ALL.|UNION ALL~UNION~INTERSECT~EXCEPT|UNION ALL concatenates result multisets.
3|Nested and complex queries|Core|A subquery is a query inside another query; correlated subqueries refer to an outer row.|Scalar subquery produces one value (standard SQL expects at most one row).~IN tests membership; EXISTS checks whether a subquery returns any rows.~ANY/SOME means at least one comparison true; ALL means every comparison true; NULL and empty sets matter.~Correlated queries depend on outer-row values.~WITH creates named common table expressions scoped to one statement.~Use NOT EXISTS with correlation to find missing matches.|SELECT name FROM Student WHERE marks>(SELECT AVG(marks) FROM Student); SELECT d.dept_name FROM Department d WHERE NOT EXISTS(SELECT 1 FROM Student s WHERE s.dept_id=d.dept_id);|If asked for second highest distinct salary, use MAX(salary) WHERE salary < (SELECT MAX(salary)...).|What does EXISTS test?|Whether a subquery returns at least one row.|At least one row exists~All rows equal~No duplicate columns~Exactly one scalar|EXISTS ignores the selected values and checks row existence.
3|Views, DCL and transaction commands|Core|A view is a named query that presents a virtual relation; access and transaction commands control privileges and changes.|CREATE VIEW v AS SELECT ...; DROP VIEW v removes definition.~Views simplify queries and restrict exposed columns; updateability depends on DBMS and query (joins/aggregates often restrict).~GRANT SELECT ON Student TO user1; REVOKE SELECT ON Student FROM user1.~COMMIT makes transaction changes permanent; ROLLBACK undoes uncommitted changes.~SAVEPOINT marks a point for partial rollback; syntax varies.|CREATE VIEW HighMarks AS SELECT name,marks FROM Student WHERE marks>=80; SELECT * FROM HighMarks;|SQLite has no GRANT/REVOKE; learn exam syntax separately. Oracle uses ROLLBACK TO savepoint_name.|Does a normal view generally store its own copy of data?|No, it stores the query definition (materialized views differ).|Query definition~Always a full copy~Only an index~Always a temporary table|Ordinary views are virtual relations.
3|Triggers (Oracle PL/SQL and SQLite)|Core|A trigger executes automatically when a specified event occurs, typically INSERT, UPDATE or DELETE.|Event-condition-action model; BEFORE and AFTER specify timing.~Row-level runs per affected row; statement-level runs per statement where supported.~Oracle uses :NEW and :OLD inside row triggers; SQLite uses NEW and OLD.~Uses: auditing, validation and derived changes; avoid unnecessary hidden side effects.~Oracle also supports system and DDL event triggers; SQLite lab supports table triggers only.|Oracle: CREATE OR REPLACE TRIGGER log_emp AFTER INSERT ON Employee FOR EACH ROW BEGIN INSERT INTO EmpLog(empname) VALUES (:NEW.empname); END; / (slash on separate line). SQLite: CREATE TRIGGER log_emp AFTER INSERT ON Employee BEGIN INSERT INTO EmpLog VALUES (NEW.empname); END;|Use the Oracle syntax from lecture for theory; do not update the same mutating table in an Oracle row trigger.|Which record refers to the inserted values in an Oracle row trigger?|:NEW.|:NEW~:OLD~:CURRENT~:INSERT|NEW holds new row values for inserts or updates.
3|Normalization and anomalies|Core|Normalization organizes relations using dependencies to reduce redundancy and update anomalies.|Insertion anomaly: cannot add a fact without unrelated data.~Deletion anomaly: deleting one fact loses another.~Update anomaly: duplicated facts must be changed consistently.~Decomposition aims for lossless join and preferably dependency preservation.~Normalization does not mean splitting every table arbitrarily.|Enrollment(SID,CID,StudentName,CourseName): names repeat. Split Student(SID,StudentName), Course(CID,CourseName), Enrollment(SID,CID).|Explain all three anomalies with a single repeated-data table.|What is a deletion anomaly?|Removing one fact unintentionally removes another needed fact.|Unintended loss of a separate fact~Duplicate primary keys only~SQL syntax error~Disk space exhaustion|Poor design can couple unrelated facts in one tuple.
3|Functional dependencies and attribute closure|PYQ|X→Y means any two tuples agreeing on X must agree on Y in every valid instance.|Trivial FD: Y⊆X; full FD: no proper subset of X determines Y.~Partial dependency: nonprime attribute depends on proper subset of candidate key.~Transitive dependency: an indirect dependency, e.g. A→B→C.~Armstrong axioms: reflexivity, augmentation, transitivity.~Closure X+ starts with X and repeatedly adds RHS when LHS is included.~A key must determine all attributes and be minimal; attributes absent from every RHS must belong to every key.|R(A,B,C,D,E,F), A→B,B→C,C→D,D→E. A+ = ABCDE; F+=F; AF+=ABCDEF. Candidate key AF.|Show closure passes and remove each key attribute to demonstrate minimality.|For R(A,B,C) with A→B and B→C, what is A+?|{A,B,C}.|ABC~AB~A~BC|Transitivity adds C after B becomes available.
3|1NF and 2NF|PYQ|1NF requires atomic attribute values; 2NF requires 1NF and no partial dependency of a nonprime attribute on any candidate key.|Atomic means one value per cell under the chosen domain.~Prime attribute belongs to at least one candidate key.~For 2NF check every candidate key, not just chosen primary key.~If every candidate key is single-attribute, 1NF implies 2NF.~Remove partial dependencies into separate relations, retaining the determinant as key.|R(SID,CID,SName,Grade), key SID,CID; SID→SName violates 2NF. Split Student(SID,SName), Enrollment(SID,CID,Grade).|PYQ AF example: A→B is partial, B nonprime; highest form 1NF assuming atomic values.|What dependency violates 2NF?|A nonprime attribute depending on a proper subset of a candidate key.|Partial dependency of nonprime attribute~Any trivial FD~Any full dependency~Any foreign key|2NF removes partial dependencies on candidate keys.
3|3NF, BCNF and decomposition|PYQ|For each nontrivial FD X→A in 3NF, X must be a superkey or A prime; BCNF requires X a superkey for every nontrivial FD.|2NF then remove problematic transitive dependencies to reach 3NF.~BCNF stricter: every nontrivial determinant is a superkey.~3NF may allow a non-superkey determinant when RHS is prime.~Binary decomposition lossless if shared attributes determine one of the two relations under F+.~Dependency preservation allows constraints checked locally; BCNF decomposition may sacrifice it.|R(A,B,C), AB→C,C→B: candidate keys AB,AC; C→B has prime RHS so 3NF but not BCNF. Decompose CB and AC (lossless).|State exact definitions before testing; do not assume every determinant must be a primary key for BCNF.|In BCNF, what must every determinant of a nontrivial FD be?|A superkey.|Superkey~Foreign key~Nonprime attribute~Chosen primary key only|BCNF permits any superkey as determinant, not just the chosen PK.
4|Transactions, states and ACID|Core|A transaction is a logical unit of database work. ACID ensures reliable execution.|Atomicity: all operations or none.~Consistency: valid state to valid state if transaction logic is correct.~Isolation: concurrent execution controlled to prevent interference according to isolation level.~Durability: committed changes survive failures.~States: active → partially committed → committed; failure → failed → aborted (restart or terminate).|Transfer ₹500 from A to B: debit and credit both succeed or both roll back; total money unchanged; committed result persists.|Define all four and apply to bank transfer; distinguish consistency from isolation.|Which property ensures committed changes survive a crash?|Durability.|Durability~Atomicity~Isolation~Consistency|Durability preserves committed updates.
4|Concurrency and common anomalies|PYQ|Concurrency interleaves transactions for throughput, but uncontrolled interleaving can violate correctness.|Lost update: T2 overwrites T1's update using old value.~Dirty read: reads uncommitted data that may roll back.~Unrepeatable read: reread same row after another committed update gets different value.~Phantom read: same predicate query returns different set after another commit.~Concurrency control coordinates execution using locks, timestamps or optimistic validation.|Balance 5000; T1 reads 5000 and adds 1000; T2 reads 5000 and subtracts 500; writes 6000 then 4500. Correct balance 5500; T1 update lost.|Draw T1/T2 schedule and show wrong vs correct output for an 8-mark answer.|Reading a value before its writer commits is called what?|Dirty read.|Dirty read~Phantom read~Deadlock~Durability|The read may observe a value that is later rolled back.
4|Schedules and conflict serializability|Core|A schedule preserves each transaction's internal order. Serial schedules run one transaction fully before another; serializable schedules are equivalent to some serial order.|Conflicts: different transactions, same item, at least one write (RW, WR, WW). RR never conflicts.~Draw a precedence graph node per transaction.~For every conflict add edge earlier transaction → later transaction.~Acyclic graph means conflict serializable; topological order gives equivalent serial order.~Cycle means not conflict serializable; may still be view serializable.|r1(A),w1(A),r2(A),w2(A) gives T1→T2 only; equivalent T1,T2. r1(A),r2(A),w1(A),w2(A) gives both directions and cycle.|List conflicting pairs and edges; do not infer conflict solely from final values.|What does a cycle in a precedence graph imply?|Not conflict serializable.|Not conflict serializable~Always serial~Always recoverable~No conflicting operations|Conflict serializability is exactly graph acyclicity.
4|View serializability and recoverable schedules|Core|View equivalence preserves initial reads, reads-from relationships and final writers for each item.|Each read of initial value must remain an initial read.~Each read from a writer must read from the same transaction's write.~Final writer for each item must match.~Every conflict-serializable schedule is view serializable; converse can fail with blind writes.~Recoverable: reader commits after writer; cascadeless: reads only committed data; strict: no reads or writes of an item written by an uncommitted other transaction.|w1(A),w2(A),w1(A) has a conflict cycle but no reads and final writer T1; view-equivalent to T2 then T1.|For view serializability test read sources and final writer, not just graph. Recoverability is a separate property.|What three things define view equivalence?|Initial reads, reads-from relations and final writers.|Initial reads, reads-from, final writes~Only final value~Only commit order~Only graph acyclicity|View equivalence compares data dependencies and final writes.
4|Locks, two-phase locking and variants|PYQ|Lock-based protocols control access using shared and exclusive locks. Two-phase locking (2PL) separates lock acquisition from release.|Shared S permits reading; multiple S compatible. Exclusive X permits read/write; conflicts with S and X from others.~Growing phase: acquire/upgrade locks; no release.~Shrinking phase: release/downgrade locks; no new acquisition.~Basic 2PL guarantees conflict serializability but may deadlock or permit cascading rollback.~Strict 2PL holds X locks until commit/abort; rigorous holds all locks; conservative acquires all required locks before execution.~Lock point is last acquisition; order lock points yields equivalent serial order.|T1: lock-S(A), read A, lock-X(B), write B, unlock A, unlock B. After unlock A, cannot acquire new locks.|Draw compatibility matrix and growing/shrinking phases; state guarantee and deadlock limitation.|What may a transaction do during shrinking phase?|Release locks, not acquire new ones.|Release locks only (no new locks)~Acquire any new locks~Never release locks~Always commit first|2PL forbids acquisition once a lock is released.
4|Deadlock and starvation|Core|Deadlock is circular waiting; starvation is indefinite delay even without a deadlock cycle.|Example T1 holds X(A), waits for B; T2 holds X(B), waits for A.~Wait-for graph edge waiter → holder; cycle indicates deadlock in ordinary lock wait-for model.~Detection: find cycle, choose victim, rollback, release locks.~Prevention: resource ordering, conservative 2PL, wait-die (older waits), wound-wait (older aborts younger).~Timeout can break waits but may abort nondeadlocked transactions.~Fair queues/aging reduce starvation.|T1→T2 and T2→T1: abort one transaction, release its locks, restart fairly.|Do not confuse wait-for graph (deadlock) and precedence graph (serializability).|What distinguishes starvation from deadlock?|Starvation can occur without circular waiting.|No circular wait is required~Always requires a cycle~Always means data loss~Always means successful commit|A transaction may be repeatedly delayed by others.
4|Timestamp ordering protocol|Core|Each transaction has a unique ordered timestamp TS. Each item X tracks read timestamp RTS(X) and write timestamp WTS(X).|Read X: if TS(T)<WTS(X), abort; otherwise read and RTS=max(RTS,TS).~Write X: if TS(T)<RTS(X) or TS(T)<WTS(X), abort under basic timestamp ordering.~Otherwise write and set WTS=TS(T).~Timestamp order ensures conflict serializability; no lock waits, so no lock deadlocks.~Repeated aborts can cause starvation; Thomas write rule may ignore obsolete writes (extra).|TS(T1)=100, TS(T2)=200, TS(T3)=300. r1(A) sets RTS=100; r3(A) sets RTS=300; w2(A) aborts because 200<300.|Make a step table of RTS/WTS and accept/reject for every operation; abort means skip later operations of that transaction.|If TS(T)=10 and WTS(X)=20, is read(X) allowed?|No; abort/restart under basic timestamp ordering.|No, abort~Yes, update RTS~Yes, update WTS~Always wait|A younger writer has already written the item.
4|Optimistic concurrency control|PYQ|Optimistic control assumes conflicts are infrequent, executes on private copies and validates before publishing writes.|Read phase: read committed database and compute local changes; track read/write sets.~Validation phase: check overlapping transactions for conflicts under chosen serial order.~Write phase: if valid, apply changes; otherwise abort/restart.~No long-held data locks in read phase; validation/commit may need synchronization.~Good for read-heavy, low-conflict workloads; high contention causes wasted work and retries.~Lock-based blocks earlier; optimistic detects conflicts later.|Two users read seat availability; both tentatively reserve; first validates and commits; second validation detects conflict and retries.|For 8 marks define three phases, example, compare blocking, overhead, deadlocks, workload suitability and restart cost.|When does optimistic concurrency check for conflicts?|Validation phase.|Validation phase~Only after permanent commit~Never~Schema definition|Validation checks whether tentative changes can safely commit.
4|Database recovery management|Core|Recovery restores a correct state after transaction, system or media failure and supports atomicity/durability.|Log records include transaction start, update old/new values, commit and abort.~Write-ahead logging (WAL): log reaches stable storage before related data page; commit log durable before success.~Deferred update typically redo committed transactions, no undo of uncommitted disk writes.~Immediate update may require undo uncommitted changes and redo committed changes (simple undo/redo model).~Checkpoint limits recovery work; backups plus logs support media recovery.~Undo rolls back incomplete work; redo reapplies durable committed work.|Log: T1 writes A 100→80 and commits; T2 writes B 50→70 without commit; crash. In simple undo/redo recovery redo T1 and undo T2.|Recovery and optimistic control are supplementary because handwritten notes focus on concurrency and timestamp ordering. State recovery model before solving.|What must be written first under WAL?|The relevant log record before its data page.|Log before data page~Data page before log~Only an index~Only the view|WAL enables recovery even if dirty pages are flushed.`;
const topics=raw.split('\n').map((line,i)=>{let [unit,title,priority,definition,bullets,example,tip,question,answer,options,why]=line.split('|'); return {id:i+1,unit:+unit,title,priority,definition,bullets:bullets.split('~'),example,tip,question,answer,options:options.split('~'),why};});
// Written exam practice: model answers, not automated essay grading.
const written=[
[1,5,'Compare a file system and DBMS with an example.','Define both. Compare redundancy, consistency, access, isolation, security, integrity, atomicity and concurrent access. Example: a student address in two files can disagree; a shared DBMS maintains one authoritative record. Mention cost/administration as limitation.','Use a two-column table and a practical example.'],
[1,5,'Explain the three levels of data abstraction and both kinds of data independence.','External/view: user-specific data; conceptual/logical: complete database structure; internal/physical: files/indexes. Logical independence separates conceptual from external; physical separates internal from conceptual. Add an index without changing tables (physical); add an attribute without changing existing views (logical).','Draw View → Logical → Physical with labels between levels.'],
[1,5,'Explain the hierarchical data model with advantages and limitations.','A rooted tree of records; each non-root child has exactly one parent, a parent may have many children. College → Department → Student. Advantages: simple structure, predictable path and fast traversal for parent-child data. Limitations: inflexible M:N, potential duplication, navigational queries and schema changes can affect applications.','Draw a tree with at least two departments.'],
[1,5,'Explain query processor and storage manager with architecture.','Users/interfaces → Query processor (DDL interpreter, DML compiler/optimizer, evaluation engine) → Storage manager (buffer, file, transaction, authorization/integrity) → Disk (data, indices, catalog, statistics). DDL records definitions; optimizer plans; engine executes; buffer caches pages; transaction manager ensures correctness.','Label arrows and subcomponents.'],
[2,7,'Explain key, domain and referential integrity constraints.','Constraints restrict valid states. Key: no two tuples have same candidate key (RollNo unique). Domain: permitted atomic types/ranges (marks 0–100). Referential integrity: each non-null FK must reference an existing eligible key (Enrollment.StudentID references Student.ID); invalid 999 rejected if absent. Entity integrity: PK not NULL. Illustrate with CREATE TABLE and CHECK/FK.','Definition and example for each type.'],
[2,5,'STUDENT(ID,RollNo,Email,Name); ID, RollNo and Email independently unique. Give candidate, primary, alternate, composite and superkey examples.','Candidate keys {ID}, {RollNo}, {Email}. Choose ID as stable primary key; other two alternate. {ID,Name} and {ID,Email} superkeys but not candidate keys. No minimal composite candidate key follows from these assumptions; a separate ENROLLMENT(SID,CID) can have composite key if each pairing unique.','Justify minimality; do not call a nonminimal combination candidate.'],
[2,10,'Design Hospital ER diagram using HOSPITAL, DOCTOR, PATIENT, MEDICAL_RECORD. Identify keys.','Assume each doctor works in one hospital; each record belongs to one patient and is authored by one doctor; hospital stores many records via its doctors. HOSPITAL(HID PK,Name,City), DOCTOR(DID PK,Name,Specialty,HID FK), PATIENT(PID PK,Name,DOB), MEDICAL_RECORD(RID PK,PID FK,DID FK,VisitDate,Diagnosis). HOSPITAL 1:N DOCTOR; DOCTOR 1:N MEDICAL_RECORD; PATIENT 1:N MEDICAL_RECORD. Every record participates totally in both links; doctor total in employment assumption. Doctor/patient M:N over visits through records.','State assumptions; draw entities, key ovals, diamonds and 1:N labels.'],
[2,10,'Convert academic ER model: Student M:N Course; Course N:1 Professor.','STUDENT(S_ID PK,S_Name,Email,Age); PROFESSOR(P_ID PK,P_Name,Email,Dept); COURSE(C_ID PK,C_Name,P_ID FK→PROFESSOR); ENROLLED_IN(S_ID FK→STUDENT,C_ID FK→COURSE,PK(S_ID,C_ID)). If C_Prof is a separate independent attribute retain it; if it denotes professor use P_ID only to avoid redundancy. M:N needs bridge, N:1 puts P_ID in COURSE. Assume one enrollment per pair.','Show PK/FK for each relation; justify with cardinality.'],
[2,7,'A={1,2,3}, B={3,4,5}. Find union, intersection, A−B, B−A and product size.','A∪B={1,2,3,4,5}; A∩B={3}; A−B={1,2}; B−A={4,5}; |A×B|=9. Rename attributes if same name before product.','Difference is directional.'],
[2,7,'Student(RollNo,Name,Dept,Marks): (101,Asha,EXTC,85),(102,Rahul,IT,72),(103,Sneha,EXTC,90),(104,Amit,CS,65). Find EXTC names, distinct departments and rename Student to S.','π_Name(σ_Dept=EXTC(Student)) = {Asha,Sneha}; π_Dept(Student) = {EXTC,IT,CS}; ρ_S(Student) returns same tuples under new name. Use quotes for string constant.','Show output column headers and tuples.'],
[3,7,'Write commands to create a Student table with ID key, required name, marks from 0 to 100, and department FK.','CREATE TABLE Student(ID INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, Name VARCHAR(40) NOT NULL, Marks INTEGER NOT NULL CHECK(Marks BETWEEN 0 AND 100), DeptID INTEGER REFERENCES Department(DeptID)); Assume Department(DeptID) already exists. INSERT INTO Student VALUES(1,\'Asha\',85,10); invalid marks 101 rejected.','Use NOT NULL plus CHECK for mandatory range.'],
[3,5,'Explain WHERE vs HAVING and COUNT(*) vs COUNT(column).','WHERE filters rows before grouping. HAVING filters groups after grouping. SELECT dept,AVG(marks) FROM Student WHERE marks>=40 GROUP BY dept HAVING AVG(marks)>75; COUNT(*) counts all rows, COUNT(marks) counts non-null marks.','Write a query with both clauses.'],
[3,7,'Explain different SQL joins with examples.','For Student s and Department d: INNER JOIN returns matches; LEFT JOIN preserves every s; RIGHT preserves every d; FULL preserves both sides; CROSS all pairs; SELF joins two aliases of same table. Use ON s.dept_id=d.dept_id, e.g. SELECT s.name,d.dept_name FROM Student s LEFT JOIN Department d ON s.dept_id=d.dept_id;. Unmatched columns NULL. NATURAL matches all common names.','Show two small input tables and resulting rows.'],
[3,5,'Explain views and triggers with an Oracle row trigger example.','View is stored query definition: CREATE VIEW HighMarks AS SELECT name,marks FROM Student WHERE marks>=80;. Trigger is automatic event-driven action. CREATE OR REPLACE TRIGGER log_emp AFTER INSERT ON Employee FOR EACH ROW BEGIN INSERT INTO EmpLog(empname) VALUES (:NEW.empname); END; / (slash on separate line). Uses auditing/validation; row trigger :OLD/:NEW; BEFORE/AFTER.','Separate the view definition from the trigger action.'],
[3,7,'R(A,B,C,D,E,F), FDs A→B, B→C, C→D, D→E. Find candidate key and highest normal form.','A,F absent from RHS, so mandatory in all keys. (AF)+=AF→ABF→ABCF→ABCDF→ABCDEF. A+ = ABCDE; F+=F; hence AF minimal and unique candidate key. Prime A,F. A→B has nonprime RHS and proper subset determinant, violating 2NF. Assuming atomic values, highest 1NF.','Show closure and minimality explicitly.'],
[3,7,'R(A,B,C,D) with A→B, B→C, A→D. Find highest NF and decompose to 3NF.','A absent RHS; A+=ABCD. Key A. With atomic values and only single-attribute candidate key, 2NF. B→C violates 3NF because B not superkey and C nonprime. Decompose R1(B,C), R2(A,B,D); intersection B determines R1, lossless; dependencies preserved. Both relations BCNF under projected dependencies.','Identify key before checking dependencies.'],
[3,7,'R(A,B,C) with AB→C, C→B. Identify keys and prove whether in 3NF/BCNF.','A mandatory; AB+=ABC and AC+=ABC. Keys AB and AC; all attributes prime. AB→C determinant superkey; C→B determinant not superkey but RHS prime, so 3NF not BCNF. Decompose R1(C,B), R2(A,C); intersection C determines R1, lossless; AB→C not locally preserved.','Classic 3NF but not BCNF example.'],
[3,7,'Normalize ORDER(OrderID,ProductID,CustomerID,CustomerName,ProductName,Qty) with key (OrderID,ProductID), OrderID→CustomerID, CustomerID→CustomerName, ProductID→ProductName.','Assume (OrderID,ProductID)→Qty. Partial OrderID→CustomerID and ProductID→ProductName violate 2NF. Split Orders(OrderID,CustomerID,CustomerName), Product(ProductID,ProductName), OrderLine(OrderID,ProductID,Qty). Remove transitive CustomerID→CustomerName to Customer(CustomerID,CustomerName), Orders(OrderID,CustomerID). All determinants keys in resulting tables under stated FDs.','Track primary and foreign keys after each decomposition.'],
[4,8,'Explain concurrency anomalies with a bank example.','Concurrent execution improves throughput but can interfere. Lost update: both read 5000; T1 adds 1000 writes 6000; T2 subtracts 500 writes 4500, correct 5500. Dirty read: T2 reads T1 uncommitted 4000; T1 rolls back to 5000. Unrepeatable read: T1 reads 5000, T2 updates/commits 6000, T1 rereads 6000. Phantom: predicate returns new/deleted matching rows. Locks/timestamps/validation control interference.','Draw operations in separate transaction columns.'],
[4,8,'Explain 2PL, compatibility and why serializability is guaranteed.','S locks allow concurrent reads; X excludes other locks. Compatibility S/S yes, S/X no, X/S no, X/X no. Growing phase acquire, no release; shrinking release, no acquire. Lock point is last acquisition. Conflicting operations order their lock points consistently, producing an acyclic precedence graph and equivalent serial order. Basic 2PL may deadlock; strict 2PL holds X locks until commit/abort.','Include example and graph of number of locks versus time.'],
[4,8,'Explain optimistic concurrency control and compare it with lock-based control.','Optimistic: read phase private work; validation checks overlapping read/write sets; write phase commits if validation passes, otherwise rollback/retry. Lock-based acquires S/X locks before access, may block/deadlock. Optimistic avoids prolonged lock waits, but restarts cost work under high contention; good for low conflict/read-heavy workloads. Locking better under some high-conflict workloads. Example two seat reservation attempts, second fails validation.','Use phase diagram and comparison table.'],
[4,7,'Determine conflict serializability: r1(A), r2(B), w1(B), w2(A).','r1(A) before w2(A): T1→T2. r2(B) before w1(B): T2→T1. Graph has directed cycle, not conflict serializable.','List conflict pairs and draw edges.'],
[4,7,'Determine view serializability of w1(A),w2(A),w1(A).','No reads, so initial read and reads-from tests vacuously satisfied. Final writer T1. Serial order T2 then T1 has same final writer. View serializable but conflict graph has T1→T2 and T2→T1; not conflict serializable.','Blind writes can make view serializability broader.'],
[4,7,'Timestamp ordering: TS1=100, TS2=200, TS3=300; r1(A),r2(B),w1(C),r3(B),r1(C),w2(B),w3(A). Start RTS/WTS all zero.','r1(A): RTS A=100. r2(B): RTS B=200. w1(C): WTS C=100. r3(B): RTS B=300. r1(C): allowed TS1=WTS C, RTS C=100. w2(B): abort (200<300). w3(A): allowed WTS A=300. Final RTS A=100,B=300,C=100; WTS A=300,B=0,C=100 in simple operation trace.','For each read compare WTS, for write compare RTS and WTS.'],
[4,5,'Compare deadlock and starvation; show wait-for graph.','Deadlock: circular wait e.g. T1 holds A wants B; T2 holds B wants A. T1→T2→T1. Detect cycle, abort victim. Starvation: transaction waits indefinitely because others repeatedly get priority, may have no cycle; prevent with fair scheduling/aging. Resource ordering prevents circular wait.','Graph arrows waiter→holder.'],
[4,7,'Explain recovery using log, WAL, checkpoint, undo and redo.','Log tracks start, updates (old/new), commit/abort. WAL forces update log before data page and commit record durable before reporting success. Checkpoint limits log scan. In simple immediate-update undo/redo model, undo incomplete transactions in reverse order; redo committed writes in log order as required. Example T1 commits A 100→80; T2 uncommitted B 50→70; recover A=80, B=50. Backup + log needed for media failure.','Mention recovery model; not all engines use identical algorithms.']
].map((r,i)=>({id:i+1,unit:r[0],marks:r[1],q:r[2],a:r[3],hint:r[4]}));
